.Dd September 4, 2026
.Dt OES 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm oes
.Nd OpenEndpointSecurity event monitoring and authorization framework
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver as a module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
oes_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
The module can only be loaded during boot and cannot be unloaded.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
framework provides endpoint-security event monitoring and authorization,
inspired by Apple's Endpoint Security API but built on the
.Fx
MAC framework and the Capsicum capability model.
It registers a MAC policy whose hooks turn kernel security-relevant operations
into events, and exposes them to userland clients through the
.Pa /dev/oes
character device.
The
.Xr liboes 3
library is the supported client interface.
The kernel interface is LP64-only.
It is unavailable to 32-bit kernels and processes, and no 32-bit ioctl
translation ABI is provided.
.Pp
Events are of two kinds.
.Bl -tag -width "NOTIFY" -offset indent
.It AUTH
Authorization events are delivered from sleepable MAC check hooks; a subscribed
client may allow or deny the operation, and the operation blocks until the
client responds or the message deadline expires.
.It NOTIFY
Notification events are informational and never block the originating thread.
When a NOTIFY event is the counterpart of an AUTH event, it is delivered after
the combined authorization decision and includes that applied decision.
Such an AUTH-derived notification is emitted before the MAC check returns; it
reports the authorization outcome, not proof that the system call subsequently
succeeded.
This differs from a post-operation notification.
.El
.Pp
Multiple clients, in different processes, may be active at once.
Each
.Xr open 2
of
.Pa /dev/oes
creates an independent client with its own subscription set, muting state, mode,
and decision cache; events fan out to every subscribed client.
The maximum number of concurrent clients is bounded by the
.Va security.oes.max_clients
tunable.
.Pp
A client subscribes to event types with the
.Dv OES_IOC_SUBSCRIBE
ioctl, reads events with
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2 ,
or
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
and answers authorization events by writing an
.Vt oes_response_t
or
.Vt oes_response_flags_t .
Processes and paths may be muted per client so that their events are suppressed.
Path muting matches the primary event object; target-path muting matches a
secondary or destination object.
It does not mean "events originating from
this executable" as similarly named APIs on some other systems do.
.Pp
A client may set
.Dv OES_SCOPE_DESCENDANTS
before setting its mode or subscriptions.
Such a client receives NOTIFY events for its opener and AUTH plus NOTIFY events
for the opener's entire descendant subtree, including descendants that already
exist when the scope is selected.
All other processes are invisible, and process mute operations are rejected
for processes outside the visible subtree.
The default self mute is not applied to this scope because it would suppress
the required root-process notifications.
.Pp
Per-event AUTH deadlines may be configured with
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_DEADLINE_MAX
and queried with
.Dv OES_IOC_GET_DEADLINE_MAX .
Descendants-scoped clients may additionally set and query deadline floors with
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_DEADLINE_MIN
and
.Dv OES_IOC_GET_DEADLINE_MIN .
A zero SET value removes the event override.
If a minimum exceeds the current maximum, the effective maximum rises to the
minimum; lowering a maximum below its minimum lowers the minimum as well.
The client-wide deadline in
.Vt oes_mode_args
is the inherited default.
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_DEADLINE_MISS_MODE
selects fail-open or fail-closed behavior for deadline misses and AUTH messages
that cannot be queued.
.Ss Event metadata
Each message contains monotonic and wall-clock timestamps, the triggering
thread ID and name when available, and a snapshot of the source process.
Process metadata includes current and
.Fx
real-parent IDs, process reaper,
process group and session, credentials and supplementary groups, audit session
and terminal data, jail and login-class identity, ABI, start time, process
state, thread count, nice value, OS release, and routing FIB.
The
.Dv EP_FLAG_OES_CLIENT
bit reports whether the source process owns an active OES client, analogous to
Apple's
.Va es_process_t.is_es_client
field.
.Pp
The executable path is captured during a successful exec and carried in the
credential label, so subsequent MAC hooks do not perform unsafe vnode path
lookups.
The path is inherited across fork and credential changes.
A process that predates OES activation may have
.Dv OES_PROC_META_PATH_UNAVAILABLE
set until its next exec.
Current-working-directory paths are also explicitly marked unavailable when
they cannot be captured without violating MAC-hook lock constraints.
.Pp
File objects include vnode identity, ownership, mode and flags, size, exact
allocated bytes, preferred block size, generation, special-device ID,
filesystem revision and type, link count, and second-plus-nanosecond access,
modification, change, and birth timestamps.
The
.Dv OES_FILE_META_*
bits distinguish unavailable attributes, unavailable or truncated paths, and
proposed attributes for objects that do not exist yet.
.Ss MAC hooks
Events are generated from MAC policy hooks, including process execution and
credential changes, the file lifecycle
.Pq open, close, create, unlink, rename, link ,
memory mapping, extended attributes and ACLs, mounts, module load and unload,
and socket and pipe operations.
File
.Em close
events are delivered via the
.Fn mac_vnode_check_close
hook; because a close cannot be denied, close is a NOTIFY-only event.
.Ss DTrace
The framework defines an
.Nm
SDT provider with probes for authorization outcomes
.Pq allow, deny, timeout ,
event enqueue and drop, and decision-cache hit and miss.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va security.oes.max_clients
Maximum number of concurrent clients.
.It Va security.oes.require_auth_clients
When non-zero, operations that would generate an AUTH event are denied if no
client is subscribed to authorize them.
.It Va security.oes.default_deadline_ms
Default client-wide AUTH deadline in milliseconds.
.It Va security.oes.default_deadline_miss_mode
Default deadline-miss behavior: zero is fail open and one is fail closed.
.It Va security.oes.default_self_mute
When non-zero, a new client is automatically muted against its own process.
This default is mutable client state and may be removed; it is not applied to
descendants-scoped clients.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /dev/oes" -compact
.It Pa /dev/oes
client device
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr liboes 3 ,
.Xr mac 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
framework first appeared in
.Fx 15.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard .
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
A client that subscribes to AUTH events can block or deny the corresponding
system operations; with
.Va security.oes.require_auth_clients
set, the absence of a client can deny operations outright.
Setting a client's deadline-miss mode to
.Dv OES_DEADLINE_MISS_FAIL_CLOSED
also denies an AUTH operation if that client's queue is full and the request
cannot be delivered.
Access to
.Pa /dev/oes
should therefore be restricted to trusted, privileged programs.
.Pp
AUTH events are delivered from MAC check hooks, several of which
.Pq for example vnode open, read, and write
run with the target vnode locked.
While a subscribed client is deciding, the originating thread sleeps in the
hook with that lock held, up to the message deadline.
The process that opened an AUTH client is implicitly allowed and is never sent
its own AUTH requests, preventing direct handler recursion.
Helper processes
are separate identities and should be explicitly muted before they perform
work on behalf of an AUTH handler; otherwise they can still deadlock on a vnode
held by the operation awaiting authorization.
Prefer NOTIFY subscriptions where blocking authorization is not required, keep
AUTH handlers fast, and set bounded default and per-event deadlines.
